All of the above data could be collected by fairly rudimentary script functions. While working on recording the stats provided when transforming a file of MARC records to RDA and loading them into the triplestore, a framework for automating the whole process developed.

Note: there are two versions of the 'stats' file: 1) the published one, encoded as JSON, and 2) an administrative one, which is saved in a system log. The difference is that file path information is stripped from the published version.

## 'RUNT' ##

To transform and add a single file to the triplestore, I developed the following script on the server:

	runt.pl [marcxml filename] [output foldername] [log foldername]

'runt' (an acronym for 'RUN Transformation') is a perl script; three parameters are required:
	MARCXML source  (eg. 'uw-5-0818.mrc.ta.xml')
	RDF results folder (a location where GraphDB can pick them up)
	Log folder (a location for the logs produced by the processing)

A unique job ID is generated as soon as 'runt' begins--it identifies the whole processing job and survives through the various stages: MARC2RDA → RDF → graphDB import.

Runt provides the following processing:
- validate required parameters: input file, output folder, log folder
- assign a unique job identifier (eg. job_id: b3fb6750bfad58ff)
- [scan the MARCXML file for UTF-8 encoding errors]
- compose a java/saxon command to run the transformation XSLT
- set a base IRI for the transform
- run the transform
- check the transform exit_code
- if no errors, gather stats and write them to a text file log (exhibit A)
- encode stats as JSON and copy that to private location (explained below)
- mail link to the JSON stats file URL to an admin address
- [scan the RDF result for IRI errors]
- copy RDF result file to GraphDB's import queue
 
From this queue a triplestore admin can import the file using the Workbench interface. When we have more confidence in the process, this import task can be added to the automation stack (via the REST API).


## THE SHELL WRAPPER (AND THE 100%) ##

Since the project sources are derived from a significant number of files (33 in the case of M2R-PROJECT), a 'wrapper' was used to specify the file list, and then to iterate each file in the project.

(A wrapper is a shell script that embeds a system command or utility, that accepts and passes a set of parameters to that command. Wrapping a script around a complex command-line simplifies invoking it. --The Linux Documentation Project https://tldp.org/LDP/abs/html/index.html). 

The wrapper manifested the following automation plan:

. Instantiate the parameters needed by 'runt' as constant values
. Add each identified MARC source filename (33 files)
. Iterate the source files--hand off each to 'runt' for processing
. Print a synopsis to the terminal for each file

Constant values are added to the shell wrapper--

INPATH="sources"
OUTPATH="imports"
LOGPATH="work"
LOG="$LOGPATH/runt-batch-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S).log"

(in practice these values contain very long file paths)

--and passed from there to 'runt' for each processing job:

runt.pl "$INPATH/nz1.mrc.ta.xml" "$OUTPATH" "$LOGPATH"
runt.pl "$INPATH/nz2.mrc.ta.xml" "$OUTPATH" "$LOGPATH"
runt.pl "$INPATH/nz3.mrc.ta.xml" "$OUTPATH" "$LOGPATH"
...
etc

and end with a per-file synopsis:

##############################################
Processing: Books.All.2016_00006p.mrc.ta.xml
JOB_ID: ec9e23eaf1bc2156
SKIPLOG: Books.All.2016_00006p.mrc.ta.xml_err.log
STATS: Books.All.2016_00006p.mrc.ta.xml.stats
JSON: ec9e23eaf1bc2156.json
LOADLINK: https://marc2rda.info/gdb_load.html?job_id=ec9e23eaf1bc2156
#############################################

SKIPLOG--log of records skipped by the transformation (due to errors)
STATS--the stats generated by 'runt' (example above)
JSON--the stats encoded as JSON and output to a file
LOADLINK--link to the administrative interface for reviewing the JSON stats.

(LOADLINK/JSON described more fully in the next section.)

I tested this process by re-running all of the files in M2R-PROJECT through the 'dev' branch of the transform. By using 'dev', which currently transforms all categories (i.e. it does not ignore certain categories of work or manifestation), this test also achieved another goal--pushing 100% of the M2R-PROJECT sources through to the triplestore. 

The repository used for this '100%' is named 'M2R-PROJECT-ALL-CATEGORIES'


## PRESERVING THE RESULTS ##

A problem yet to be resolved is what to do with the resulting stats; they should remain available to whoever might want to view them. Up to this point our batch processing has created a hodge-podge of stats, in various degrees of completeness, stored in a variety of folders on a windows computer. Now that the processing and the corresponding stats were becoming standardized and automated, a predictable and accessible storage regime was required.

My solution was to save these stats into the same repository into which the RDA/RDF data is being loaded. 

To convert stats to RDF triples: the job ID is used to construct the subject IRI; the stats names become predicates; and the stats values become literals.
	
For example, these stats--

job_id: b3fb6750bfad58ff
transform_started: Sun 20 Sep 06:54:26 EDT 2026
marcxml_source_file: uw-5-0818.mrc.ta.xml
marcxml_file_size: 799688820
...

--become these triples:

<rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/b3fb6750bfad58ff">
    <ex:m2r_job_id>b3fb6750bfad58ff</ex:m2r_job_id>
    <ex:m2r_transform_started>Sun 20 Sep 06:54:26 EDT 2026</ex:m2r_transform_started>    
    <ex:m2r_marcxml_source_file>uw-5-0818.mrc.ta.xml</ex:m2r_marcxml_source_file>
    <ex:m2r_marcxml_file_size>800M</ex:m2r_marcxml_file_size>
...

Regarding the domain, I just grabbed what was to hand, so to speak. As for the properties, my goal was to avoid creating data that could accidentally be retrieved in a user query. (I'm open to suggestions for both parts). One imperfection in this solution is that the RDF stats describe a transformation job but are not directly linked to the individual triples produced by that job.. (A straightforward way of doing that would be to 'tag' each imported MARC2RDA triple with the jobId of the MARC source, thus creating a quad. But I'm uneasy about adding that extra weight to the triples, not only with regard to storage.) 

Regarding the JSON/LOADLINK mentioned above, the JSON file is stored in a private server directory and is not directly accessible from the web. Instead, the `LOADLINK` provides a URL to a load-params script, with the unique `job_id` identifying the JSON file associated with that run. When the administrator opens the link, the script uses the `job_id` to locate and read the JSON file, then passes its contents to the web interface for review and editing. This allows the administrator to work with the data--presented as a two-column table--through a browser without exposing the JSON file itself to direct web access. If the result meets with the viewer's (admin) approval, a SAVE button converts the JSON data to RDF; then, optionally, imports the RDF stats into the repository. 

The import of the stats RDF could be automated using the API, but I decided some cursory admin approval would be welcome: keeping the stats import separate allows for problems in the MARC2RDA pipeline to be identified and resolved before any (possibly inaccurate) stats are committed to the triplestore--it adds a deliberate boundary between the metrics being collected and the data imported to the repository.


